//
// tests/nonblock_actual_dma_test.cpp
//
// Bounded DIAGNOSTIC software around tests/nonblock_actual_dma_top.sv.
//
// What is REAL here (no C++ fakes):
//   * AW / AR / W on the IOMMU translation-request port are produced by TWO
//     real dma_core_wrap (frontend + legalizer + R->AW coupler + datapath);
//   * the frontend register file is reached through a REAL AXI slave access
//     generated by the test-only register-master FSM in the SV wrapper: the
//     C++ only issues cfg_valid/cfg_write/cfg_addr/cfg_wdata/cfg_wstrb.
//
// What is MODELED (identical to tests/nonblock_coupled_test.cpp):
//   * DDR + page tables + one ordered R-return queue (completion + DDTW + PTW);
//   * a per-4KiB page content checker.
//
// Software timeline (all phase prints are software-timing diagnostics and are
// NOT a board measurement):
//   1. setup_memory()/reset() per case (counters never carried across cases);
//   2. warm: --warmup V transfers CONCURRENT with a continuously refilled A
//      ring over --a-pages pages, batch OFF on both.  This models the Linux
//      warm driver ORDER (linux/drivers/misc/idma.c:2497-2528 plus
//      idma_run_window() at idma.c:689-748): A launched first, V second;
//      each loop iteration polls and refills V before A; V stops at exactly
//      --warmup completions; the A transfer left open by that stop is
//      drained.  It does NOT model absolute board timing.  The continuous
//      autonomous-A overlap during the measurement window is still
//      exercised by the batch phase below, not here;
//   3. configure A autonomous batch (target --a-target) and V batch
//      (target --transfers, pages=1, gap --v-gap), both configurations
//      completed before any trigger;
//   4. A NEXT_ID trigger, then V NEXT_ID trigger;
//   5. wait V batch complete, STOP A, drain to quiet;
//   6. verify every touched page byte-for-byte; check quiet.
//
// Warm-phase model limitations (no board-equivalence claim):
//   * the model exercises the driver ORDER (A first, V second; V-first poll,
//     A-second poll; exactly --warmup V completions; one A ring page per
//     submit; final A drain).  It does NOT model absolute CPU instruction,
//     cache or interrupt timing: every register access here is a full
//     multi-cycle cfg AXI transaction issued by a single-threaded software
//     loop, so cycle counts are model numbers, not board numbers;
//   * both DMAs are polled by that one loop: no concurrent CPU, no IRQ
//     latency and no multi-core contention is modelled;
//   * the warm A refill count depends on model timing, so it is reported
//     by the WARM-SUMMARY line and asserted only to be > 0, never to a
//     board value.
//
// Exit: 0 on PASS, non-zero on FAIL / bounded STALL / WAIT.

